body:
On `57464f4ec`, a caller can register path-based file removal with `TaskGroup::add_defer`. The equivalent cleanup using an open directory handle fails to type-check. A directory handle is useful here because it continues to identify the intended directory if the directory is renamed before cleanup.

```moonbit
async test "directory-relative cleanup" {
  let dir = @fs.opendir(".")
  defer dir.close()
  @async.with_task_group() <| group => {
    group.add_defer(() => dir.remove("output.tmp", allow_missing=true))
  }
}
```

On the native target the compiler reports E4227 at `dir.remove(...)` because `add_defer` expects an `async () -> Unit nocancel` callback. Replacing the call with `@fs.remove("output.tmp", allow_missing=true)` type-checks.

Please declare `Directory::remove` as `-> Unit nocancel`, regenerate the package interface, and cover the directory-relative call in a cleanup test. Both removal APIs already delegate to the same `nocancel` helper. I verified that adding the annotation alone lets a directory-relative cleanup callback type-check. Filesystem errors can still propagate.

[TaskGroup::add_defer signature](https://github.com/moonbitlang/core/blob/57464f4ec/async/pkg.generated.mbti#L89) · [Path-based removal and shared helper](https://github.com/moonbitlang/core/blob/57464f4ec/async/fs/dir.mbt#L73-L98) · [Directory-relative removal](https://github.com/moonbitlang/core/blob/57464f4ec/async/fs/dir.mbt#L361-L374)

## Summary

Reading from `@io.MemoryReader` (or the in-memory `@io.pipe()`) moves at most 1016 bytes per `read()`, and every transfer costs one scheduler round. On the js target a round that has newly woken tasks is deferred with `setTimeout(0)`, which Node clamps to about 1 ms. So reading 2.6 MB takes about 3 seconds on js, almost all of it waiting: the process uses 0.18 s of CPU in 5 s of wall time. Native is also slower than it needs to be (35 ms for 2.6 MB, about 75 MB/s).

## Reproduction

```moonbit
// moon.pkg: import "moonbitlang/core/async", "moonbitlang/core/async/io",
// "moonbitlang/core/bench"; pkgtype(kind: "executable");
// options("allow-experimental-packages": true)

///|
async fn run(chunks : Int, size : Int) -> Unit {
  let data = Bytes::make(size, b'x')
  let start = @bench.monotonic_clock_start()
  let reader = @io.MemoryReader(w => for _ in 0..<chunks {
    w.write(data)
  })
  defer reader.close()
  let mut total = 0
  let mut reads = 0
  let mut largest = 0
  while reader.read() is Some(b) {
    total += b.length()
    reads += 1
    largest = largest.max(b.length())
  }
  let ms = @bench.monotonic_clock_end(start) / 1000.0
  println("chunks=\{chunks} size=\{size}: \{ms} ms, \{total} bytes, reads=\{reads} largest=\{largest}")
}

///|
async fn main {
  for size in [1024, 16384, 65536] {
    for chunks in [10, 40] {
      run(chunks, size)
    }
  }
}
```

`moon run --target js --release cmd/main` and `--target native`, Apple Silicon, moon 0.1.20261104, moonc v0.10.20 (2026-11-05), Node v25.8.2:

| writes | bytes | reads | largest read | js | native |
| ------ | ----- | ----- | ------------ | -- | ------ |
| 10 x 1 KiB  | 10 KiB  | 20   | 1016 | 30 ms   | 0.3 ms |
| 40 x 1 KiB  | 40 KiB  | 80   | 1016 | 96 ms   | 1.1 ms |
| 10 x 16 KiB | 160 KiB | 170  | 1016 | 200 ms  | 2.3 ms |
| 40 x 16 KiB | 640 KiB | 680  | 1016 | 781 ms  | 9.1 ms |
| 10 x 64 KiB | 640 KiB | 650  | 1016 | 739 ms  | 8.9 ms |
| 40 x 64 KiB | 2.5 MiB | 2600 | 1016 | 2968 ms | 35 ms  |

Each 1 KiB write takes two reads (1016 + 8 bytes). On js every `read()` costs 1.1 to 1.5 ms, however the writer chunks the data. `/usr/bin/time` for the whole js run: real 5.0 s, user 0.18 s. Same result on core main (70af5a09c) with a moonc built from compiler master.

## Cause

1. **Every read is at most one 1 KiB segment.** `BinaryReader::read` calls `peek`, which calls `ensure_buffered(1)`. `BinaryReaderController::enlarge_to` (async/io/binary_reader_controller.mbt, `SEGMENT_SIZE = 1024`) grows the empty buffer to one segment, so `raw_read_impl` is called with `max_len` of about 1016. `PipeRead::raw_read_impl` (async/io/pipe.mbt) copies `min(max_len, writer.len)`, wakes the writer and does `@coroutine.pause_nocancel()`. A 64 KiB `write` is therefore delivered in 64 rounds.
2. **On js, each such round goes through `setTimeout(0)`.** `reschedule` in async/internal/event_loop/event_loop.js.mbt runs one round of ready coroutines and, if more became ready, does `set_timeout(0, reschedule)`. Node clamps `setTimeout(0)` to 1 ms (browsers clamp to 4 ms after 5 nested timers), so a ping-pong between a reader and a writer runs at about 1000 rounds per second.

## Suggestions

- Deliver a pipe write in one read when possible: for example, size the buffer for `read()`/`peek()` to the pending writer's remaining length (or a larger default, such as 64 KiB, as `read_all`'s `chunk_size` suggests), or give `PipeRead`/`MemoryReader` a `read()` that returns the writer's remaining slice directly.
- On js, run immediately ready tasks without a timer clamp: `setImmediate` on Node, or a `MessageChannel` post in browsers, and keep starvation protection with a time or round budget before falling back to `setTimeout`.

Either change alone removes most of the gap; the first also helps native.

Found while benchmarking a streaming JSON parser (https://github.com/bobzhang/ajson) that reads bytes through `MemoryReader`: on js, the reader took about 3 s of a 3.2 s parse of 2.4 MB, against 141 ms when the same parser is fed string chunks directly.

## Summary

On the native backend, each call to `@bench.monotonic_clock_start()` allocates 32 bytes that are never freed.
Calling `monotonic_clock_end` on the timestamp does not free it either.
A program that times every request with it grows without bound.

## Environment

- moon 0.1.20261023 (3778465 2026-10-23)
- moonc v0.10.17+d971670cd (2026-10-23)
- moonrun 0.1.20261023 (3778465 2026-10-23)
- Target: native, both `--release` and `--debug`
- OS: Linux aarch64 (Asahi Linux)

It also happens with moonc v0.10.14+7d59c7ec9 and moon 0.1.20260920 (native, `--release`).

## Reproduction

`moon.mod`:

```
name = "repro/clockleak"

preferred_target = "native"
```

`main/moon.pkg`:

```
import {
  "moonbitlang/core/bench",
  "moonbitlang/core/env",
}

options(
  "is-main": true,
  "native-stub": [ "rss.c" ],
)
```

`main/rss.c` reads VmRSS from `/proc/self/status`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

// VmRSS of /proc/self/status, in KiB.
int repro_rss_kib(void) {
  FILE *f = fopen("/proc/self/status", "r");
  char line[256];
  int kib = -1;
  if (!f) return -1;
  while (fgets(line, sizeof line, f)) {
    if (strncmp(line, "VmRSS:", 6) == 0) { sscanf(line + 6, "%d", &kib); break; }
  }
  fclose(f);
  return kib;
}
```

`main/main.mbt`:

```mbt
///|
extern "C" fn rss_kib() -> Int32 = "repro_rss_kib"

///|
fn main {
  let args = @env.args()
  let mode = if args.length() > 1 { args[1] } else { "leak" }
  let n = 1_000_000
  let before = rss_kib()
  let mut sum = 0.0
  match mode {
    // Call start and drop the result.
    "leak" =>
      for _ in 0..<n {
        ignore(@bench.monotonic_clock_start())
      }
    // The usual pattern: start, then end on the same timestamp.
    "start_end" =>
      for _ in 0..<n {
        let t = @bench.monotonic_clock_start()
        sum += @bench.monotonic_clock_end(t)
      }
    // Workaround: one origin, read with end.
    "workaround" => {
      let origin = @bench.monotonic_clock_start()
      for _ in 0..<n {
        sum += @bench.monotonic_clock_end(origin)
      }
    }
    _ => abort("unknown mode")
  }
  let after = rss_kib()
  let per_call = (after - before).to_double() * 1024.0 / n.to_double()
  println(
    "\{mode}: VmRSS \{before} KiB -> \{after} KiB (+\{after - before} KiB), \{per_call} B/call, sum>=0: \{sum >= 0.0}",
  )
}
```

Run each mode with `--release` and with `--debug`:

```
moon run main --target native --release -- leak
moon run main --target native --release -- start_end
moon run main --target native --release -- workaround
```

## Expected

VmRSS stays flat. Each `Timestamp` is dropped after its iteration.

## Actual

Growth of VmRSS over 1,000,000 iterations:

| Mode | `--release` | `--debug` |
|---|---|---|
| `leak` (start only) | +31,504 KiB, 32.3 B/call | +31,520 KiB, 32.3 B/call |
| `start_end` (start, then end) | +31,520 KiB, 32.3 B/call | +31,536 KiB, 32.3 B/call |
| `workaround` (one origin) | +256 KiB, 0.26 B/call | +272 KiB, 0.28 B/call |

## Workaround

Call `monotonic_clock_start()` once, at startup, and keep the result as an origin.
Read times with `monotonic_clock_end(origin)` and subtract.

```mbt
let origin = @bench.monotonic_clock_start() // once
let t0 = @bench.monotonic_clock_end(origin)
// ... work ...
let elapsed_us = @bench.monotonic_clock_end(origin) - t0
```

## Cause

From reading the source on `main` and the generated C:

- core declares the type as `#external type Timestamp` ([bench/monotonic_clock_native.mbt#L16-L17](https://github.com/moonbitlang/core/blob/df5289b8fc98d31258b507abca24bff45cae555d/bench/monotonic_clock_native.mbt#L16-L17)).
  An `#external` value is a raw pointer, so MoonBit never increfs or decrefs it.
- The runtime allocates the timestamp with `moonbit_make_external_object` ([env.c#L812-L819](https://github.com/moonbitlang/moonbit-native-runtime/blob/88e2a69ffff2f3faae07d2a94a9f7e54808cc2d1/env.c#L812-L819)).
  That is a reference-counted object: an 8-byte header, a 16-byte `timespec`, and an 8-byte finalizer pointer, 32 bytes in all.
  Only `moonbit_decref` can free it.
- The generated C for `start_end` has no `moonbit_decref` on the timestamp:

```c
void* _M0L1tS918 = _M0FPC15bench23monotonic__clock__start();
double _M0L6_2atmpS1104 = _M0Lm3sumS914;
double _M0L6_2atmpS1105 =
  _M0FPC15bench21monotonic__clock__end(_M0L1tS918);
```

So nothing ever frees it.
The Windows branch ([env.c#L777-L782](https://github.com/moonbitlang/moonbit-native-runtime/blob/88e2a69ffff2f3faae07d2a94a9f7e54808cc2d1/env.c#L777-L782)) allocates the same way, so I expect it to leak too.
I tested only native on Linux. I did not test Windows, wasm, wasm-gc, or JS.

Possible fixes (untested):

- Drop `#external`, so that MoonBit reference-counts `Timestamp`. This matches what the runtime allocates. `monotonic_clock_end` would need to borrow `ts`.
- Or make the timestamp a plain integer (for example, nanoseconds as `UInt64`), so that nothing is allocated.

On the native backend, an object is never freed after its own `Array` field has held a closure that captures the object, and that element was then removed with `Array::clear` or `Array::pop`. The closure is never freed either, along with everything it captures. If the element is overwritten with `arr[i] = ...` instead, the memory is released. Storing the closure in an `Option` field and resetting the field to `None` also releases it.

`clear` and `pop` both go through `UninitializedArray::set_null` (`%fixedarray.set_null`), and both leak. Indexed assignment does not leak.

### Reproduction

`moon.mod`: `name = "zz/selfleak"`, `source = "src"`. `src/moon.pkg` imports `moonbitlang/core/env` and sets `"is-main": true`.

```moonbit
struct Box {
  items : Array[() -> Unit]
  mut one : (() -> Unit)?
  pad : FixedArray[Double]
}

let sum : Ref[Double] = Ref(0.0)

fn main {
  let mode = match @env.args().get(1) {
    Some(m) => m
    None => "array"
  }
  for i in 0..<100 {
    let b : Box = { items: [], one: None, pad: FixedArray::make(1000000, 1.0) }
    match mode {
      // a closure that reads `b`, in an array `b` holds, then emptied
      "array" => {
        b.items.push(fn() { sum.val = sum.val + b.pad[5] })
        b.items.clear()
      }
      // the same, taken out by pop
      "pop" => {
        b.items.push(fn() { sum.val = sum.val + b.pad[5] })
        b.items.pop() |> ignore
      }
      // the same, overwritten by one that reads nothing
      "overwrite" => {
        b.items.push(fn() { sum.val = sum.val + b.pad[5] })
        b.items[0] = fn() {  }
      }
      // a closure that reads nothing of `b`, in `b`'s array
      "other" => {
        let p = FixedArray::make(10, 1.0)
        b.items.push(fn() { sum.val = sum.val + p[5] })
        b.items.clear()
      }
      // a closure that reads `b`, in an array of its own
      "local" => {
        let a : Array[() -> Unit] = []
        a.push(fn() { sum.val = sum.val + b.pad[5] })
        a.clear()
      }
      // a closure that reads `b`, in an Option field of `b`
      "option" => {
        b.one = Some(fn() { sum.val = sum.val + b.pad[5] })
        b.one = None
      }
      _ => sum.val = sum.val + b.pad[5]
    }
  }
  println(sum.val)
}
```

Each iteration allocates an 8 MB `pad`. The peak resident set size after 100 iterations (`/usr/bin/time -l`, macOS arm64):

| mode | debug | release |
|---|---|---|
| none | 10 MB | 10 MB |
| array (`clear`) | 768 MB | 768 MB |
| pop | 768 MB | 768 MB |
| overwrite | 10 MB | 10 MB |
| other | 10 MB | 10 MB |
| local | 10 MB | 10 MB |
| option | 10 MB | 10 MB |

Every `Box` survives in the `clear` and `pop` cases, which is about 8 MB × 100.

### Versions

```
moon 0.1.20261102 (ea07287 2026-11-02)
moonc v0.10.19+bee578715 (2026-11-02)
```

### Where we hit it

This is in a UI framework. A per-window context keeps arrays of callbacks that capture the context, and they are cleared every frame. Every test window's context leaked, about 2-7 MB each. A test process that opens about 2000 windows grew to about 9 GB.

Found while reviewing #4431 at commit `b8f106888fe25a7fa95153fe0ec19d7c49b77a05`. Tracking separately for a later fix.

`BinaryReader::read_into` validates only `max_len > 0`. With an empty reader buffer, it forwards `offset` and `max_len` to `raw_read_impl` without checking the destination array bounds. For a native file reader, these values reach `pread` as `buf + offset` and `len`, allowing a write outside the array.

Source: [binary_reader.mbt, lines 110–125](https://github.com/moonbitlang/core/blob/b8f106888fe25a7fa95153fe0ec19d7c49b77a05/async_io/io/binary_reader.mbt#L110-L125).

### Reproduction

Run this as an async test on the native target, importing `moonbitlang/core/async_io` and `moonbitlang/core/async_io/fs`. The working directory must contain a nonempty `moon.mod`.

```moonbit
async test "reject read outside destination" {
  let file = @fs.open("moon.mod", mode=ReadOnly)
  defer file.close()
  let dst : FixedArray[Byte] = FixedArray::make(16, b'0')
  try file.read_into(dst, offset=16, max_len=1) catch {
    _ => ()
  } noraise {
    n => fail("out-of-bounds read succeeded with \{n} byte(s)")
  }
}
```

Observed on macOS/native: `out-of-bounds read succeeded with 1 byte(s)`. The destination has no capacity at offset 16, so the successful read writes outside its logical allocation. This is a memory-safety issue, not just an incorrect return value.

### Expected behavior

Reject invalid destination ranges before performing I/O. Check that `0 <= offset <= dst.length()` and `max_len <= dst.length() - offset`, retaining the positive-length requirement. Use subtraction after validating the offset to avoid overflow in `offset + max_len`.

Add regression coverage for negative offsets, offsets past the end, and lengths exceeding the remaining capacity. Audit sibling native entry points such as `File::read_at` and UDP receive methods for the same unchecked-range pattern.

Validated with Moon 0.1.20261021 and moonc v0.10.17+093cb8f9c.

I hit this while porting [typify](https://github.com/oxidecomputer/typify) to MoonBit ([bobzhang/typify](https://mooncakes.io/docs/bobzhang/typify)). Its generated MoonBit types must accept and reject exactly the same JSON as the serde-derived Rust types. Two properties of the builtin `Json` made that impossible, so the port carries its own ordered document type.

Repro on moon 0.1.20260920:

```moonbit
test {
  // 1. Duplicate keys: the last value wins, silently.
  let j = @json.parse("{\"b\":1,\"a\":2,\"b\":3}")
  inspect(j.stringify(), content="{\"b\":3,\"a\":2}")

  // 2. The float spelling is lost: `1.0` and `1` are the same value, with no repr.
  debug_inspect(@json.parse("1.0"), content="Number(1)")
  debug_inspect(@json.parse("1"), content="Number(1)")
}
```

Why it matters for interop:
- **Duplicate keys.** serde rejects duplicates of a struct's named fields (`duplicate field ...`), but maps keep the last value. Validating strictly, or even detecting duplicates, needs the parser to report them. Once parsed into `Json`, the information is gone.
- **Integers vs floats.** serde_json keeps them apart: `1.0` into a `u64` field is an error (`invalid type: floating point 1.0, expected u64`), and serde_json writes `f64` values back as `1.0`. `repr` is kept for integers too large for a `Double` (e.g. `18446744073709551615`), but not for integral floats. So `Json` can't round-trip `1.0` or tell it from `1`.

Possible directions, any one of which would be enough:
- a `parse` option to fail on duplicate keys, or a streaming/event API that exposes them;
- keeping `repr` (or an `is_float` flag) whenever the literal has a fraction or exponent;
- or documenting that `Json` is a value model, not a lossless wire model.

Related: #2650 (`Eq` ignores `repr`), #4053 (`-0` loses its sign; still reproduces on 0.1.20260920), #1366 (number representation).

### Description

The non-JS implementation of `Double::round` / `@math.round` is incorrect in several binary64 boundary regions:

1. It loses negative zero.
2. It rounds `nextDown(0.5)` to `1.0`.
3. It changes already-integral values in `[2^52, 2^53)`.

The JS target produces the expected results, while native and wasm-gc exhibit all three problems.

### Minimal regression tests

```moonbit
test "round preserves negative zero" {
  assert_eq(
    @math.round(-0.0).reinterpret_as_uint64(),
    0x8000000000000000UL,
  )

  assert_eq(
    @math.round(-0.25).reinterpret_as_uint64(),
    0x8000000000000000UL,
  )
}

test "round does not cross the half boundary" {
  let input = 0x3fdfffffffffffffUL.reinterpret_as_double()
  // input == nextDown(0.5)

  assert_eq(
    @math.round(input).reinterpret_as_uint64(),
    0x0000000000000000UL,
  )
}

test "round is the identity on representable integers" {
  let input_bits = 0x4330000000000001UL
  // input == 4503599627370497.0 == 2^52 + 1

  assert_eq(
    @math
      .round(input_bits.reinterpret_as_double())
      .reinterpret_as_uint64(),
    input_bits,
  )
}
```

These tests pass on JS and fail on native and wasm-gc.

### Cross-target results

| Input | Expected/JS | native/wasm-gc |
|---|---|---|
| `-0.0` | `-0.0`, bits `0x8000000000000000` | `+0.0`, bits `0x0000000000000000` |
| `-0.25` | `-0.0`, bits `0x8000000000000000` | `+0.0`, bits `0x0000000000000000` |
| `nextDown(0.5)` | `0.0`, bits `0x0000000000000000` | `1.0`, bits `0x3ff0000000000000` |
| `2^52 + 1` | bits `0x4330000000000001` | bits `0x4330000000000002` |

### Expected behavior

Under the documented “round half up” rule:

- Negative inputs in `[-0.5, 0)` should produce negative zero.
- Every value strictly below `0.5` should round to zero.
- Any exactly representable integer must be a fixed point of `round`.

Raw-bit assertions are needed for the first property because ordinary floating-point equality does not distinguish `-0.0` from `+0.0`.

**Background**

The `Logger` trait in `builtin/traits.mbt` defines `write_view` and `write_substring` with mutually recursive default implementations.

This pattern appears to be intended to allow implementers to provide either method, with the other derived automatically—similar to how `Show` relies on `output` and `to_string`.

**And**

Unlike `Show`, which uses `#must_implement_one(output, to_string)` to enforce that at least one of its mutually defaulted methods is implemented, `Logger` has no equivalent enforcement for `write_view` and `write_substring`. 

**But**

A type can implement `Logger` while overriding **neither** `write_view` nor `write_substring`. Such code compiles successfully. A deprecation warning is produced for `impl write_substring`.

Calling `write_string`, `write_view`, or `write_substring` on an instance of that type then triggers infinite mutual recursion between the two default implementations, causing a stack overflow at runtime.

Minimal reproduction:

```moonbit
struct MyLogger {
  mut buf : String
}

impl Logger for MyLogger with write_char(self, c) {
  self.buf = self.buf + c.to_string()
}

fn main {
  let l = MyLogger::{ buf: "" }
  l.write_string("hello")
  println("Result: " + l.buf)
}
```

Running this with `moon run` compiles successfully but crashes with a stack overflow.

## Summary

A `for state = init { ... }` loop whose body is shaped as

```moonbit
for state = init {
  if async_or_effectful_cond(state) {
    break value
  }
  // fallthrough — no `else`
  continue next_state(state)
}
```

**hangs (non-terminates)** when the condition expression actually suspends (e.g. via `@async.sleep` or another `async` call inside the `if` predicate). Wrapping the same control flow in either an `else` branch *that performs the continue logic* or in a `match` arm with a guard makes it terminate. The control-flow shapes are semantically identical to a human reader, so this looks like a miscompilation in async lowering of `for` + `if`-without-else (or with `else { () }`) when the condition has a suspension point.

## Environment

```
moon  0.1.20260427 (48d7def 2026-04-27)
moonc v0.9.1+cd5b07232 (2026-04-28)
moonrun 0.1.20260427 (48d7def 2026-04-27)
Feature flags: rr_moon_pkg
Target: native (also reproduces in CI on ubuntu-latest and macos-latest)
```

Project: https://github.com/moonbitlang/maria — branch `hongbo/fix-loop-syntax`, commit before `64168802` (the workaround).

## Minimal repro

Drop this into a package that already imports `moonbitlang/async` (e.g. `internal/git/` in `moonbitlang/maria`):

```moonbit
///|
async fn _probe(s : String) -> Bool {
  @async.sleep(1)
  s == "/a"
}

///|
fn _step_up(s : String) -> String {
  match s {
    "/a/b" => "/a"
    "/a" => "/"
    "/" => "/"
    _ => "/"
  }
}

///|
/// Form A — HANGS.
async fn _find_buggy(start : String) -> String? {
  for dir = start {
    if _probe(dir) {
      break Some(dir)
    }
    let p = _step_up(dir)
    if p == dir || p == "." || p.is_empty() {
      break None
    } else {
      continue p
    }
  }
}

///|
/// Form B — passes.
async fn _find_match(start : String) -> String? {
  for dir = start {
    match dir {
      dir if _probe(dir) => break Some(dir)
      dir => {
        let p = _step_up(dir)
        if p == dir || p == "." || p.is_empty() {
          break None
        } else {
          continue p
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

///|
async test "match form" {
  assert_eq(_find_match("/a/b"), Some("/a"))
}

///|
async test "buggy if form (hangs)" {
  assert_eq(_find_buggy("/a/b"), Some("/a"))
}
```

Run:

```
moon test <pkg> -f "*match form*"        # passes immediately
moon test <pkg> -f "*buggy if form*"     # hangs indefinitely (kill with timeout)
```

## Variants tested (in maria's `internal/git/repo_root.mbt`)

| Form | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| Original `loop start_dir { dir if cond => Some(dir); dir => { ... } }` | passes |
| `for` + `if cond { break Some(dir) }` then fallthrough to parent/continue logic | **HANGS** |
| `for` + `if cond { break Some(dir) }` with explicit `else { () }` then fallthrough | **HANGS** |
| `for` + `if cond { break Some(dir) } else { /* parent + continue here */ }` | passes |
| `for` + `match dir { dir if cond => break Some(dir); dir => { ... } }` | passes |
| Same Form A but `cond` does NOT suspend (`async fn` that just returns a Bool with no `@async.sleep`) | passes |

The last row is important: removing the suspension point from the condition makes Form A pass, even though it's still inside an `async fn`. The trigger is specifically a real suspension inside the `if` predicate of the `for`-body, with the false branch as fallthrough.

## Why this matters

The new `for state = init { ... }` syntax is what the deprecation hint for the legacy functional `loop` syntax tells users to migrate to. Mechanical rewrites of the form

```moonbit
loop init {
  state if async_pred(state) => result
  state => { ... ; continue next }
}
```

into

```moonbit
for state = init {
  if async_pred(state) { break result }
  ...
  continue next
}
```

silently miscompile. The match-arm form is a workaround but is heavier and doesn't match the deprecation hint's example. We hit this in maria when migrating ~42 functional loops; bisect identified the call site (`find_repo_root`) by reverting a single file.

## Real-world impact

In maria, the buggy `for`-shape caused `internal/skills` tests to hang for >17 minutes in CI before timing out. The hang was not visible in standalone repros until we forced a real suspension in the condition (per @codex-cli's analysis), explaining why it was hard to bisect.

## Workarounds

1. Move the false path into an explicit `else` branch (no fallthrough).
2. Use `match` with a guarded arm.
3. Avoid suspending in the condition expression — bind the boolean first: `let hit = async_pred(state); if hit { break X }`.

## Suggested fix area

Async coroutine lowering of `for` loops — specifically the case where the body contains an `if` whose condition has a suspension point and whose true branch ends in `break`. The "fallthrough to next statement" continuation appears to be lost or rewired to itself when state-machine-ifying the suspension.

Happy to provide additional repros or test variants. The maria branch with the original buggy commit and the workaround is at https://github.com/moonbitlang/maria/pull/955 (commits before/after `64168802`).

## Summary

`Bench::keep()` is intended to prevent the compiler from optimizing away benchmark results, but its implementation heap-allocates a `Ref` and constructs a trait object on every call. This adds ~6 ns of overhead per iteration on top of the existing closure dispatch cost, severely inflating measurements for fast operations.

## Reproduction

```moonbit
// bench_wbtest.mbt

struct Point { x : Int; y : Int } derive(Show)

fn make_point() -> Point {
  { x: 42, y: 99 }
}

test (b : @bench.T) {
  // With keep: ~10 ns
  b.bench(name="with_keep", fn() { b.keep(make_point()) })

  // With let _: ~3.5 ns
  b.bench(name="with_let", fn() { let _ = make_point() })
}
```

`make_point()` is trivial (~1 ns), but `b.keep()` inflates it to ~10 ns. Using `let _ =` instead gives ~3.5 ns (remaining cost is closure dispatch overhead, see #3407).

## Root cause

`Bench::keep()` implementation:

```moonbit
pub fn[Any] Bench::keep(self : Bench, value : Any) -> Unit {
  let trait_object : &OpaqueValue = @ref.new(value)
  self._storage = trait_object
}
```

Every call does:
1. **`@ref.new(value)`** — heap-allocates a `Ref[T]` wrapper to box the value
2. **Trait object construction** — creates an `OpaqueValue` trait object (vtable + pointer)
3. **`moonbit_decref`** on the previous `_storage` value — frees the old `Ref`
4. **Store** the new trait object into `self._storage`

From the generated C (per call):

```c
// 1. Heap-allocate Ref[T]
ref_ptr = ref_new(value);

// 2. Construct trait object
trait_object = { vtable_id, ref_ptr };

// 3. Decref old storage
old = self->storage;
if (old.ptr) moonbit_decref(old.ptr);

// 4. Store new
self->storage = trait_object;

// 5. Decref self (bench object)
moonbit_decref(self);
```

This is **5 operations** (including a heap allocation and deallocation) executed on every benchmark iteration, inside the timed region.

## Overhead breakdown

Measured on x86-64 Linux:

| Approach | Time per iteration | Overhead |
|----------|-------------------|----------|
| `b.keep(result)` | ~10 ns | ~6 ns from keep + ~3 ns closure dispatch |
| `sink = result` (mutable local) | ~7 ns | ~3 ns from sink RC + ~3 ns closure dispatch |
| `let _ = result` | ~3.5 ns | ~3 ns closure dispatch only |
| Actual operation | ~1 ns | — |

## Comparison with Go

Go's equivalent is assigning to a package-level variable:

```go
var sink Point

func BenchmarkMakePoint(b *testing.B) {
    for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
        sink = makePoint()  // single store, no allocation
    }
}
```

This is a single memory store — no heap allocation, no reference counting, no trait object construction.

## Suggestion

`keep()` should prevent dead-code elimination without heap-allocating. Possible approaches:

- **Volatile store**: Write the value to a volatile memory location that the compiler cannot optimize away, without wrapping it in a `Ref`.
- **Compiler intrinsic**: A `#[no_optimize]` annotation or `black_box()` intrinsic (similar to Rust's `std::hint::black_box`) that marks a value as observable without any runtime cost.
- **Opaque identity function**: An `extern` function that takes and returns a value, preventing the compiler from reasoning about whether it's used, with zero actual overhead.

## Summary

`b.bench()` measures closure dispatch overhead in addition to the benchmarked code, making it unreliable for sub-10 ns operations. For fast operations (~1-2 ns), the closure overhead (~3 ns) dominates the measurement.

## Reproduction

```moonbit
// bench_wbtest.mbt
test (b : @bench.T) {
  let arr : FixedArray[Byte] = FixedArray::make(100, b'\x42')
  b.bench(name="array_read", fn() { let _ = arr[50] })

  let mut x : Byte = b'\x00'
  b.bench(name="byte_add", fn() { x = x + b'\x01' })
  ignore(x)
}
```

Expected: both operations should measure < 1 ns (a single array read or byte add).
Actual: both report ~3 ns, because the closure dispatch cost floors the measurement.

## Root cause

`iter_n_nanoseconds` calls the closure inside the timed region:

```moonbit
fn iter_n_nanoseconds(inner : () -> Unit, k : Int) -> Double {
  let ts = monotonic_clock_start()
  for _ in 0..<k {
    inner()   // closure dispatch overhead included in timing
  }
  let diff = monotonic_clock_end(ts)
  diff
}
```

Each `inner()` call compiles to (from generated C):

```c
while (i < k) {
    moonbit_incref(inner);        // RC increment on closure
    inner->code(inner);           // indirect call through function pointer
    // inside closure body:
    //   env->rc -= 1             // RC on closure environment
    //   moonbit_incref(captured_var)  // RC on each captured variable
    //   << actual benchmarked code >>
    //   moonbit_decref(captured_var)  // RC cleanup
    i++;
}
```

Per iteration, the overhead is:
- `moonbit_incref` on the closure object
- Indirect function call (branch predictor unfriendly)
- Closure environment RC manipulation
- `moonbit_incref`/`moonbit_decref` on each captured variable

This adds ~3 ns per iteration on a modern x86 machine. For operations that take microseconds or more (codec decode, image draw), this overhead is negligible. But for operations in the 1-10 ns range (pixel access, arithmetic, array indexing), the overhead is comparable to or larger than the actual work, making the measurements unreliable.

### Attempted mitigations

1. **`#borrow(inner)` on `iter_n_nanoseconds`**: Removes the final `moonbit_decref` on the closure after the loop, but the per-iteration `moonbit_incref` and the closure body's own RC ops remain.

2. **`#borrow(f)` on `Bench::bench`**: Does not propagate transitively to `iter_n_nanoseconds`.

3. **`b.keep()` vs `let _ =`**: `b.keep()` adds even more overhead (~6 ns extra) because it heap-allocates a `Ref` and constructs a trait object on every call. Using `let _ =` avoids this, but the closure dispatch overhead remains.

## Comparison with Go

Go's `testing.B` benchmark loop compiles to a direct inline loop:

```go
func BenchmarkArrayRead(b *testing.B) {
    arr := make([]byte, 100)
    for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
        _ = arr[50]   // inlined, no closure, no RC
    }
}
```

No closures, no function pointers, no reference counting. The compiler inlines the operation directly into the loop body, so the measurement reflects only the benchmarked code.

## Suggestion

The fix likely needs to happen at the compiler/runtime level. Some possible approaches:

- **Elide RC on non-escaping closures**: The closure passed to `iter_n_nanoseconds` never escapes (not stored, not returned), so the compiler could skip RC operations entirely.
- **Inline the closure into the loop**: Monomorphize/specialize `iter_n_nanoseconds` so the closure body is inlined into the loop, eliminating the indirect call.
- **Provide a compiler-level benchmark primitive**: A built-in `@bench` mechanism that expands at compile time rather than using runtime closures.

## Summary

`Int` provides `min`, `max`, and `clamp` methods, but `Float`, `Double`, `Int64`, and `UInt` do not. Similarly, `Double` has `signum` but `Float` does not. This creates an inconsistency that forces users to hand-roll these common utilities.

## Missing methods

| Method | `Int` | `Float` | `Double` | `Int64` | `UInt` |
|--------|:-----:|:-------:|:--------:|:-------:|:------:|
| `min`  | ✅    | ❌      | ❌       | ❌      | ❌     |
| `max`  | ✅    | ❌      | ❌       | ❌      | ❌     |
| `clamp`| ✅    | ❌      | ❌       | ❌      | ❌     |
| `signum`| —    | ❌      | ✅       | —       | —      |

## Motivation

While refactoring ~150 raylib game examples written in MoonBit, we found that nearly every game had to hand-roll `minf`/`maxf`/`clampf` helpers like:

```moonbit
fn clampf(v : Float, lo : Float, hi : Float) -> Float {
  if v < lo { lo } else if v > hi { hi } else { v }
}

fn minf(a : Float, b : Float) -> Float {
  if a < b { a } else { b }
}

fn maxf(a : Float, b : Float) -> Float {
  if a > b { a } else { b }
}

fn signf(v : Float) -> Float {
  if v > 0.0 { 1.0 } else if v < 0.0 { -1.0 } else { 0.0 }
}
```

These could not be eliminated because `Float` lacks the corresponding core library methods that `Int` already provides. We were able to replace `mini`/`maxi`/`clampi`/`absi` with `Int::min`/`Int::max`/`Int::clamp`/`Int::abs`, but the `Float` and `Double` equivalents do not exist.

## Expected API

```moonbit
// Float
pub fn Float::min(self : Float, other : Float) -> Float
pub fn Float::max(self : Float, other : Float) -> Float
pub fn Float::clamp(self : Float, min~ : Float, max~ : Float) -> Float
pub fn Float::signum(self : Float) -> Float  // parity with Double::signum

// Double
pub fn Double::min(self : Double, other : Double) -> Double
pub fn Double::max(self : Double, other : Double) -> Double
pub fn Double::clamp(self : Double, min~ : Double, max~ : Double) -> Double

// Int64, UInt (lower priority but good for consistency)
pub fn Int64::min(self : Int64, other : Int64) -> Int64
pub fn Int64::max(self : Int64, other : Int64) -> Int64
pub fn Int64::clamp(self : Int64, min~ : Int64, max~ : Int64) -> Int64
pub fn UInt::min(self : UInt, other : UInt) -> UInt
pub fn UInt::max(self : UInt, other : UInt) -> UInt
pub fn UInt::clamp(self : UInt, min~ : UInt, max~ : UInt) -> UInt
```

### Note on NaN semantics for Float/Double

For `min`/`max`, the IEEE 754 `fmin`/`fmax` semantics (propagate non-NaN, i.e. `min(NaN, 1.0) == 1.0`) would be the most useful default, matching Rust's `f32::min`/`f64::min` and C's `fmin`/`fmax`.

