**Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.**
The current `inspect` function design requires users to supply both the value being printed and the expected value at the same callsite. This might hinder certain use cases where the `inspect` needs to be called mid-procedure in a helper function.

The current inspect function also suffers from argument order issues, and can be piped into, requiring many heuristics in `moon` to correctly determine the position of the content to be added or replaced, for example:

https://github.com/moonbitlang/moon/blob/8ab6c9e516b29dbc249252b13e478393e24bfcc2/crates/moonbuild/src/expect.rs#L499-L615

**Describe the solution you'd like**
Split the `inspect` function into two parts: A type representing the expected value waiting to be inspected, and a method to inspect the value at any place you want:

```moonbit
pub struct Expected {
  content: String
  loc: SourceLoc
}

pub fn expect(content?: String, loc~ : @builtin.SourceLoc, args_loc~ : @builtin.ArgsLoc) -> Expected {
  { content, loc: args_loc[0] }
}

pub fn Expected::assert_equal(self: Expected, value: &Show) -> Unit {
  // ...check equality, print update instructions...
}
```

So the value can be used anywhere, even mid-test:

```moonbit
fn aux(input: String, e1: Expected, e2: Expected) -> Unit{
  e1.assert_equal(input)
  let output = // do something
  e2.assert_equal(output)
}

test {
  aux("example", expect("warrrgh"), expect("nya"))
}

test {
  aux("another", expect("now you see me"), expect("soon you won't"))
}
```

This design is completely unpipable, because it accepts no positional arguments, thus requiring no heuristic to determine argument positions. It also won't require the value and expectation to share a single call site, making it more versatile to be used in helper functions.

**Describe alternatives you've considered**
N/A

**Additional context**
Referenced Rust's [expect_test](https://docs.rs/expect_test), which uses the latter way and makes it easy to update values mid-procedure.

